<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Regnenses</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Regni or Regnenses were the people of the Roman civitas centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noviomagus-reginorum">Noviomagus Reginorum</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a>, in what is now Sussex. The area formed part of the client kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togidubnus">Togidubnus</a> after the Roman conquest of 43. Their name may mean the people of the kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regni.jpg" alt="Regni" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.south.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Regni, also called the Regini, were a Celtic tribe or group of tribes living before the Roman conquest in what is now Sussex and small parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, Surrey and Kent. Their neighbours were the Belgae to the west, the Atrebates to the north and the Cantiaci to the east. Ptolemy calls them Regni, with a single city, Noiomagus, and some scholars prefer that name or the Brythonic Regini to Regnenses. A comparison with other civitas capitals suggests another reading of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noviomagus-reginorum">Noviomagus Reginorum</a>, New Market, Land of the Regini.</p><p>By the end of the Middle Iron Age, about 100 BCE, the downland hillforts had been abandoned and settlements on the coastal plain had multiplied. The Regni were probably native tribes influenced by the Belgae, perhaps under a Belgic elite, though increased trade may have mattered more than migration. They were closely tied to the Atrebates, and Noviomagus served as an early capital of the Atrebates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> seem to have kept a nominally independent client kingdom, perhaps as a buffer, and the Regni are the only people its king certainly ruled; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> records that certain civitates were given to him and remarks on his loyalty. The Romans made heavy use of Chichester Harbour right after 43 to supply their legions in the south-west. After the king's death around 80 the region became a civitas of the province, governed from Noviomagus, with ports, villas and roads such as the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> later called Stane Street.</p><p>In the late 3rd century the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javols">Anderitum</a>, modern Pevensey, was built in the civitas against raids across the Channel. After Roman rule ended, the Kingdom of Sussex formed in the former civitas, traditionally with the arrival of King Aelle in 477, though his existence is debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Regni farmed the coastal plain, worked iron in the Weald, made textiles and salt and traded with the continent; they were probably among the British peoples most assimilated to Roman culture even before the conquest. Cremation was established in their territory by the 70s BCE, and shrines stood at Hayling Island, Lancing Down and Westhampnett. Their coins blended Roman imagery, such as the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, with goddesses of their own, and the Weald later held the third-largest iron works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, at Beauport Park near Hastings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Regni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some peoples, such as the <b>Regni</b> under Togidubnus and the Iceni under Prasutagus, were left as client kingdoms, while Vespasian, commanding the II Augusta, conquered the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its lands were divided into civitates, the <b>Regni</b> around Chichester and probably the Belgae (Britain) around Winchester.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-regni">Kingdom of the Regni</a></li></ul></section>
